<Base title="Editorial Policy" description="How GuideHollow selects topics, uses official sources and AI assistance, distinguishes examples from testing, and handles corrections." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Editorial Policy',url:'/editorial-policy/'}]}>
<header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow mint">/ editorial_policy</p><h1>Clarity starts<br />with honest limits.</h1><p>Published October 23, 2026. Updated October 25, 2026. Our standards for useful, accountable AI guides.</p></header><article class="prose info-content"><h2>Our purpose</h2><p>We publish original, practical explanations for everyday AI users. Each guide should solve a recognizable problem, explain the key idea, and give the reader something concrete to try. We prefer a useful small library over a large collection of repetitive articles.</p><h2>Sources and verification</h2><p>We prioritize official product documentation, primary research, and direct technical references. References appear at the end of each article. A source check means we reviewed the linked material; it does not mean every example was executed in every product or account.</p><p>We avoid unsupported price comparisons, model rankings, and performance claims. Features may vary by plan, region, workspace, and product updates. When a detail is uncertain, we qualify it or leave it out. Material without a verifiable factual basis stays unpublished.</p><h2>AI assistance and attribution</h2><p>Our launch content was produced with AI assistance for research organization, drafting, editing, and code implementation. Guides carry the attribution “GuideHollow Editorial.” This attribution describes responsibility for the publication and does not imply a team of named experts or personal product experience.</p><p>Fictional examples are teaching examples. We do not invent interviews, testimonials, benchmarks, credentials, or first-person testing stories. When we report a test, we identify what was actually checked and its limits.</p><h2>How to read the evidence in a guide</h2><ul><li><strong>Editorial example:</strong> an answer constructed to demonstrate a method. The <a href="/guides/how-to-draft-emails-with-ai/">email revision pair</a> shows what should be preserved; it is not a captured model run.</li><li><strong>Observed run:</strong> an output recorded from a stated interaction. The <a href="/ai-tools/getting-started-with-chatgpt/">two-turn beginner exercise</a> includes its request, response, follow-up, and run limits. One successful run is not a reliability estimate.</li><li><strong>Source check:</strong> a claim compared with an original reference. The <a href="/guides/how-to-verify-ai-generated-information/">claim ledger</a> separates supported, contradicted, and unresolved claims. A source check does not prove an account action happened.</li></ul><p>When an observed answer fails a check, the recorded output and editorial correction should stay distinguishable. For example, the <a href="/guides/how-to-summarize-long-documents-with-ai/">document summary review</a> records an overstatement about money instead of silently rewriting the captured answer.</p><h2>Reproducing a calculation</h2><p>For calculations, an article should state its inputs, formula, expected output and failure cases. Our <a href="/guides/verify-ai-weighted-average-price-spreadsheet/">weighted-price walkthrough</a> provides the original <a href="/examples/weighted-price-sample.csv">eight-row fictional CSV</a>, an <a href="/tools/weighted-price-checker/">independent in-browser calculator</a>, and two explicit error-injection tests. Readers can challenge or repeat the arithmetic without trusting an AI-generated answer. The fixture is made up for teaching; its correctness is not a claim that a model or commercial spreadsheet was tested.</p><p>For model behavior we instead provide exact requests and observed outputs when we actually have them, and report the interface, date, and limits. A simulated example cannot be substituted for an observed run, and a source link alone does not establish hands-on experience.</p><p>For prompt-evaluation tutorials, the <a href="/examples/prompt-evaluation-routing.csv">eight-case teaching dataset</a> records the editor-written expected answers and clearly labels the illustrative prediction columns. Our <a href="/prompts/test-ai-prompts-with-evaluation-dataset/">scoring walkthrough</a> explains how to reproduce its counts without presenting them as live model performance.</p><h2>Dates and maintenance</h2><p>Publication dates reflect initial preparation for publication. Updated dates change when the content materially changes. References were checked on the date stated in the article. We do not promise continuous monitoring of every third-party document.</p><h2>Corrections</h2><p>Use our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> to flag an error with the affected URL and supporting evidence. Accepted corrections are reflected in the article and public repository history. Significant corrections should explain what changed; silent cosmetic edits do not imply a new fact-check.</p><h2>Commercial independence</h2><p>No paid placement, affiliate links, or advertisements are active at launch. Future sponsorships or affiliate relationships will be disclosed near the relevant content. Advertising must remain distinct from editorial recommendations.</p><h2>Limits of our advice</h2><p>Our guides are educational. They do not replace qualified professional advice for decisions involving health, law, money, safety, or regulated work. Verify important outputs and follow the rules that apply to your organization and situation.</p></article>
</Base>
